Well, it can be to entertain the player, or it can
be to be a control freak exercise for the DevTeam.
The two aren't really compatible.

> I do, however, believe that the dev team should
> not think of everyone.

In particular, they should ignore players who are so
focused on ascending they've forgotten that the game
is perfectly playable with no thought of ascension,
and who endlessly whine if the game is not trimmed
to match only their own play-style.

> I'm even relatively certain they aren't thinking
> of everyone.

> Past pudding farming style exploits usually were
> closed off (polypiling, sink kicking, etc).

As Dylan noted for us with the article message ID,
pudding farming has now been well known for about
a dozen years, probably right back through all those
changes. It doesn't appear at all obvious that the
DevTeam are so oblivious they don't know it's there,
and approve its being there.

> The fact people enjoyed free polypiling didn't
> stop them from introducing golems.

Which are avoidable with careful play, so they don't
stop polypiling at all, and quite obviously never
had that intent. Any patience game that yields lots
of /oPoly is going to feed back into the polymorph
lifestyle with as much polypiling as the player
could possibly want to use. I've ended games with
three fully charged wands of polymorph lots of
times [since I don't happen to like polymorphing (or
alchemy), stuff high on my list of "if it were only
me playing, this could sure be left out"].

You need to grab that meme that it is _not_ "only
you playing" and chew on it until you have digested
it.  The game is fun to a much broader spectrum of
players than those presently whining piteously
because a playstyle they don't care to use can also
bear fruit.

> It may be arrogant to make a game change to
> exclude a playstyle that one sees as degenerate.

Yep, and you've put yourself fully in the pocket of
the arrogant control freaks trying to spoil fun
parts of the game for others.

> However, I would contend it is *necessary* to game
> design to make such changes.  The real risk is
> that the degenerate game styles become common
> place

Unfortunately, you insist on defining "degenerate"
as _stuff you don't like_, which is _way_ arrogant.

_My_ list of "degenerate" tactics include killing
shopkeepers, killing priests, or allowing either
to be killed by my pets, converting attended
altars, alchemy, polypiling, and a long list of
others. That doesn't mean I think the game should
be changed to deprive others of them, though.

If you don't like patience games, you _do_ have the
option not to play them. NetHack is much easier to
win by other means, like memorizing spoilers.

> and end up commandeering control of the games
> evolution.

That's meaningless noise.

> Content becomes balanced against the degenerate
> behaviour, forcing those remaining true to the
> original playstyle to also adapt or quit.

Why? You don't see me adopting to the play style of
"memorize all the spoilers, to play a game with no
errors and maximized probabilities", yet I've kept
at the game since it was first published.

A better guess is that those who are so competitive
they cannot play the game for its own sake, but only
to beat someone else at it, are _never_ going to be
happy players anyway, so altering the game to cater
to their insecurities will only impoverish the game.

> One would think I am advocating the rail-roading
> of playstyle or of gameplay.

Yep, no question about it, and pre-emptive denial
is a form of dishonesty; now the misrepresentation
game starts.

> It is rather the opposite.  The more open you want
> your game to be to different playstyles, the more
> careful you must be about the edge cases.
> Otherwise, the players will find the "key"
> pattern, exploit it, and leave the vast majority
> of your work untouched.

This isn't Doom, leaving parts of the game untouched
is perfectly acceptable.

If you think the DevTeam wants to _force_ the game
players to use all game features, you might want to
find a way to justify the existence in the game of
such optional little nothings as, say, Medusa,
Mines, the Oracle, Ft. Ludius, Sokoban. The castle
is almost optional, you can run in the back door,
dive down a hole, and take the misgained level
express to miss it on the way up, IIUC.

Fact, as opposed to your fiction, is that the
DevTeam over the years have put a huge amount of
richness into the game, with no attempt to cram that
richness down every single player's throat. Pudding
farming seems very much a part of that. When broken
stuff has been repaired, like my much beloved corner
kick in shops fixed by walls that heal themselves,
it wasn't to make robbing shops impossible, just a
bit more balanced.

And there's plain _nothing_ out of balance about
catering for patience game players, except that it
really torques the jaws of control freaks who don't
have that much patience and so insist the game
should only be winnable with the skills _they_
possess.

I don't buy that one-true-path philosophy one bit.

If there are a dozen ways to succeed at NetHack, and
you can only gag down three of them, then just cope
with your inadequacies, don't start attacking those
who can do what you cannot.

That you don't want to use some techniques is no
reason at all to deprive others of the fun of using
those methods you can't stand to use yourself.

> The players farming for their elusive spellbook
> aren't attempting other paths: such as, say,
> ascending without the necessary spellbook.

So? Later, when pudding farming wanes in attraction,
they are quite free to learn new skills. No one is
keeping score on the DevTeam to assure that they
have found a way to force every line of code in
NetHack to be exercised in each and every game.
NetHack isn't a one or two play game, for many of
us, it is a lifetime of enjoyment, exploring all the
many, many ways it can be played. What you advocate
would simply impoverish the game and deprive it of
its ability to fascinate players for years on end.

> I do agree with the original poster that the right
> place to address this concern is with Death Drop.
> Death Drop is an unfortunate hangover.  Item
> creation on death doesn't mix well with monster
> inventory systems, IMHO.

Except that the opposite approach would require
elaborate justifications for why monsters carry
around stuff like rings, but never use them, or
alternately would leave the dungeon much deprived of
such jewelry.

Death drop is a balance tool, it lets sufficient
resources enter the game without littering every
level with multiple shops, and without vastly
munging the game to provide alternate sources of
goodies.

Plain and simple, NetHack is still _a game_. There
is no requirement that everything in it satisfy a
real world logic. It is quite reasonable that some
stuff "just works that way" for game playability
reasons. As well try to abolish the grid system
because people in the real world can move in more
than ten directions.

Your scheme is not well thought out.

More to the point, it isn't based on good ethics.

